## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of provider aliases and per-environment module instantiation in Terraform.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases and module-level provider mappings
- For_each environment deployment and target verification
- Safe plan/apply separation per environment

## Code Example
```javascript
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"dev\"
  region = \"us-west-2\"
}
provider "aws" {
  alias = \"prod\"
  region = \"us-east-1\"
}
variable \"environments\" { type = \"map(string)\"; default = { prod = \"prod\"; staging = \"staging\" } }

module \"vpc\" {
  for_each = var.environments
  source   = \"./modules/vpc\"
  providers = {
    aws = each.key == \"prod\" ? aws.prod : aws.dev
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback if prod plan uses the wrong alias?
- How can you enforce alias usage at plan time to prevent mix-ups?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical use of for_each with maps, resource interdependencies, and stable identities across environments. Delivers hands-on pattern candidates expect in production.

## Key Concepts
- for_each with maps provides stable keys and predictable IDs
- referencing attributes from one resource in another (subnet_id in NAT GW)
- tagging and AZ assignment per subnet
- outputs to surface IDs for downstream resources
- when to prefer for_each vs count (stable keys vs positional indexing)

## Code Example
```javascript
variable "subnets" {
  type = map(object({ cidr_block = string; az = string }))
  default = {
    az1 = { cidr_block = "10.0.1.0/24", az = "us-east-1a" },
    az2 = { cidr_block = "10.0.2.0/24", az = "us-east-1b" },
    az3 = { cidr_block = "10.0.3.0/24", az = "us-east-1c" }
  }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "private" {
  for_each = var.subnets
  vpc_id            = var.vpc_id
  cidr_block        = each.value.cidr_block
  availability_zone = each.value.az
  tags = {
    Name = "private-${each.key}"
  }
}

output "private_subnet_ids" {
  value = [for s in aws_subnet.private : s.id]
}
```

```javascript
resource "aws_nat_gateway" "ngw" {
  for_each = aws_subnet.private
  allocation_id = aws_eip.ngw[each.key].id
  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.private[each.key].id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- When would you choose for_each over count in real projects?
- How would you migrate from count-based to for_each-based resources without breaking state?
- How would you validate AZ distribution and CIDR correctness after apply?

## Why This Is Asked

This checks practical import workflows and multi-resource management with for_each, keeping data in sync and enabling safe CI-driven promotions.

## Key Concepts

- terraform import with for_each
- map of per-bucket settings
- dynamic blocks for conditional blocks
- drift reconciliation and idempotency
- CI/CD validation and promotion

## Code Example

```javascript
variable "buckets" {
  type = map(object({
    encryption = bool
    versioning = bool
  }))
}
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  for_each = var.buckets
  bucket = each.key
  acl    = "private"

  dynamic "versioning" {
    for_each = each.value.versioning ? [1] : []
    content {
      enabled = true
    }
  }

  dynamic "server_side_encryption_configuration" {
    for_each = each.value.encryption ? [1] : []
    content {
      rule {
        apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
          sse_algorithm = "AES256"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform import 'aws_s3_bucket.bucket["team-a"]' team-a-bucket
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle a bucket rename in the map without recreating?
- How would you test encryption changes in CI without touching prod data?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world Terraform governance requires strict isolation and policy enforcement when multiple teams/environments share global resources. This question probes your ability to combine per-environment workspaces with policy guardrails to prevent cross-environment impact.

## Key Concepts
- Per-environment Terraform Cloud workspaces for isolation
- Provider aliases to target the correct AWS account
- Sentinel policy to guard changes to shared VPC components
- CI validation: parse plan JSON and enforce env-tag constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sentinel-like illustrative policy (Terraform Cloud Sentinel syntax would differ)
policy "env_guard" {
  rule deny_shared_vpc_changes {
    // deny any change to shared VPC unless resource.tags.env == workspace.name
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle tag drift across environments?
- How would you test policy changes locally before pushing to CI?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to compose remote state across envs, leverage backend aliases, and isolate region-specific DNS resources. Also assesses practical CI gating to prevent prod drift.

## Key Concepts
- Backend aliases and per-env state
- terraform_remote_state data source for cross-env outputs
- for_each with a DNS map to generate records
- CI gating with plan JSON to block prod changes

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tfstate-bucket"
    key    = "envs/region-eu/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "eu-west-1"
  }
}

data "terraform_remote_state" "prod" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "tfstate-bucket"
    key    = "envs/prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

module "dns" {
  source       = "./modules/dns"
  dns_records  = var.dns_records
  prod_zone_id = data.terraform_remote_state.prod.outputs.zone_id
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 5 regions while keeping drift detection?
- How do you handle secrets in backend configs for multi-tenant regions?
